-- Resolver primitives. Not for application code — the prelude reaches
-- into this namespace only when defining derived control combinators.
--
-- See proposal 033 (docs/proposals/033-resolver-primitives-and-disjunction.md).
namespace anthill.kernel
  import anthill.reflect.{Term}

  -- Binary choice point. Splits the current goal queue into two branches
  -- that share the frame's tail: [?a, ...tail] tried first, [?b, ...tail]
  -- on backtrack. Both branches inherit σ unchanged.
  operation push_choice(a: Term, b: Term) -> Bool

  -- Binary conjunction. The dual of `push_choice`, and simpler because there is
  -- no branch: splices BOTH goals into the current queue ahead of the tail —
  -- [?a, ?b, ...tail] — in one frame, sharing σ. `?a` and `?b` are GOALS, so an
  -- unground one is resolved (or delayed) like any other goal rather than being
  -- required to evaluate to a `Bool`.
  --
  -- This is what `and` in a goal position had been missing (WI-20260822-J38JE).
  -- `not` and `or` each had a resolver primitive to be redirected to and `and` did
  -- not, so §6.6 had to REFUSE `a & b` there and name the comma. With this the
  -- three are symmetric and the refusal is gone.
  --
  -- The conjunction reading SUBSUMES the boolean-value one wherever both apply: a
  -- `Bool` expression in goal position is a condition (§5.3), so "?a succeeds" is
  -- "?a is true", and `and(true, false)` fails as a conjunction exactly as it is
  -- false as a value.
  operation push_and(a: Term, b: Term) -> Bool

  -- Negation-as-failure: succeeds if `query` has no solutions, fails if it has
  -- one. DELAYS while `query` still contains an unbound variable — NAF over a
  -- non-ground goal is unsound, so the floundering guard rotates the goal rather
  -- than answering, and a search that merely ran out of DEPTH is undecided too,
  -- never a refutation (WI-628).
  --
  -- HERE, and not in `anthill.reflect`, since WI-20260820-MH90F. It is a resolver
  -- primitive by every test `push_choice` above passes — same shape (a reified
  -- goal as a `Term`), same `-> Bool` naming the test outcome, and its whole
  -- behaviour is an effect on the search rather than a value computed from its
  -- argument. `anthill.reflect` is the term-level face — the namespace whose
  -- members return DATA about a program, and where `unify`'s doc below sends you
  -- for the substitution its own `-> Bool` does not return. Filing the primitive
  -- there put the one member of this namespace outside it.
  --
  -- NO IMPORT NEEDED, and the header above means it — though not for the reason this
  -- comment used to give. A bare `not(x)` is the PREFIX OPERATOR (kernel-language.md
  -- §6.6), minted with the address `..anthill.kernel.not`, so it reaches this declaration
  -- without running the name ladder at all. It used to be credited to the implicit
  -- prelude (load.rs `PRELUDE_QUALIFIED`); that entry left in WI-20260826-XED22 and
  -- nothing changed, which is how the tier rung was found to have been dead. Application
  -- code still never names `anthill.kernel` to negate.
  -- Two `rules.anthill` files did import it and both were retired when the move
  -- landed — an import here buys nothing and contradicts what this namespace is for.
  --
  -- NOT the boolean `!`. `anthill.prelude.Bool.not` is a different function: it
  -- takes a Bool VALUE and is two-valued, where this takes a reified GOAL and is
  -- three-valued. The two are selected BY POSITION (kernel-language.md §6.6) — a
  -- rule-body goal means this one, an operation-body expression means `Bool.not`
  -- — and the reason it must be by position rather than by proposal 052's uniform
  -- `eq(op(args), true)` routing is written up in 052 §Open questions 7.
  operation not(query: Term) -> Bool

  -- Structural unification (proposal 049). The object-level face of `<=>`
  -- (and `let ?v = e`): resolver-implemented, carrier-agnostic, binding via a
  -- frame effect rather than a return value (parallels Eq.eq). `-> Bool` names
  -- the test outcome; the substitution it really produces is the term-level
  -- `anthill.reflect.unify` face. Never dispatches — structural only.
  --
  -- WI-1056: an OPERATION-LEVEL type parameter, for the reason `struct_eq` below
  -- already states — it is not a spec op, so nothing binds a carrier parameter for
  -- it per call. Written `(a: T, b: T)` against a NAMESPACE-level `sort T = ?`,
  -- every `<=>` in every program shared ONE symbol as its argument type, so a rule
  -- variable typed from this signature came out as the bare `T`: `rule f(?x) <=>
  -- ?y :- …, ?y = 1` then compared `1` against `T` and read as a mismatch. Nothing
  -- reported it while rule bodies went unchecked (WI-1043/WI-1056).
  --
  -- That `sort T = ?` is DELETED with this change rather than left in place: `unify`
  -- was its last user, and a namespace-scoped wildcard sort named `T` is precisely
  -- the thing a future member would pick up by writing a bare `T` — re-creating the
  -- defect silently.
  operation unify[T](a: T, b: T) -> Bool

  -- Structural identity test (proposal 051 / WI-615). The `===` operator: a
  -- total, carrier-agnostic, dispatch-free comparison of two values by raw
  -- structure (opaque carriers compare by handle identity). Resolver-implemented
  -- via the same `builtin_eq` structural comparator `=`/`eq` shortcut to today —
  -- but under its OWN symbol, so when `=`/`eq` become genuinely dispatched
  -- semantic equality (`Eq.eq`, Phase 2 / WI-616) `===` stays structural. Needs
  -- no `Eq` instance. Never dispatches. Unlike `Eq.eq` (whose `T` is the `Eq`
  -- spec's own param, bound per-call by spec-op dispatch), `struct_eq` is not a
  -- spec op, so it carries an OPERATION-LEVEL type parameter `[T]` (proposal 042)
  -- the typer binds per call — letting `a === b` type-check in an operation body.
  operation struct_eq[T](a: T, b: T) -> Bool

  -- Cut (`!`). Commits to the current rule invocation: discards the other
  -- clauses of the rule whose body the `!` appears in, and every choice point
  -- created while resolving the goals before it. Choice points created after
  -- the `!` are untouched. Surface syntax is the bare `!` goal; the resolver
  -- opens it to a barrier-tagged `cut(B)` when the rule body is entered (B is
  -- the enclosing invocation's barrier, parallel to De Bruijn-var opening), so
  -- the nullary declaration here is what the `!` RESOLVES TO, and `!` is the
  -- normal way to mean it. A written `cut()` still works UNDER AN IMPORT — the
  -- primitive is keyed on this symbol, not on a spelling — but no longer resolves
  -- bare: rustland's converter names this declaration's address, so the implicit
  -- prelude no longer carries the short name. (scaland still mints the short `cut`
  -- and defines no `anthill.kernel.cut`; the port is behind here.) Special-cased in the
  -- resolver (its effect is on the choice-point stack, not on σ), never
  -- dispatched. See proposal 033.1 (docs/proposals/033-resolver-primitives-and-disjunction.md
  -- §Future work) / WI-568.
  operation cut() -> Bool

  -- Derived disjunction. Lifts the WAM "captured continuation" idiom to a
  -- regular rule head so callers don't need direct access to the primitive.
  rule or(?a, ?b) :- push_choice(?a, ?b)

  -- Derived conjunction, the peer of `or` above. `a & b` in a rule body routes
  -- here (§6.6's position-directed booleans), so goal conjunction has a functor
  -- as well as the comma.
  rule and(?a, ?b) :- push_and(?a, ?b)
end
